      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="ten-to-four">
      <h2><span class="num">02 — What Changed</span>Ten to Four</h2>
      <p>On October 29, 2025, a little over four months after the original filing, OpenEvidence filed its amended complaint. The ten causes of action had become four: CFAA, breach of contract, unjust enrichment, and trespass to chattels. Gone were the trade-secret misappropriation claim under the DTSA, the DMCA, the Lanham Act, defamation, Massachusetts Chapter 93A, and common-law unfair competition. The defined term "OpenEvidence Trade Secrets" from the original complaint did not appear in the amended version. The trade-secret cause of action was dropped entirely. The phrase "trade secret information" did persist once, in the factual allegations: paragraph 78 of the amended complaint described Pathway's prompt injection attacks as an attempt to extract "OpenEvidence's confidential proprietary and trade secret information." But no cause of action rested on that language.</p>
      <p>No filing explains why six causes of action were dropped.</p>
      <div class="oe-redline-wrap">
        <div class="oe-redline-header">Causes of Action: Dkt 1 (June 20, 2025) vs. Dkt 38 (Oct. 29, 2025)</div>
        <div class="oe-redline-body">
          <div class="oe-redline-item oe-redline-dropped">
            <span class="oe-redline-num">1.</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-name">DTSA Trade Secret Misappropriation</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-tag">Dropped</span>
          </div>
          <div class="oe-redline-item oe-redline-kept">
            <span class="oe-redline-num">2.</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-name">Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA)</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-tag">Kept &rarr; Count 1</span>
          </div>
          <div class="oe-redline-item oe-redline-dropped">
            <span class="oe-redline-num">3.</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-name">DMCA</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-tag">Dropped</span>
          </div>
          <div class="oe-redline-item oe-redline-kept">
            <span class="oe-redline-num">4.</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-name">Breach of Contract</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-tag">Kept &rarr; Count 2</span>
          </div>
          <div class="oe-redline-item oe-redline-kept">
            <span class="oe-redline-num">5.</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-name">Unjust Enrichment</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-tag">Kept &rarr; Count 3</span>
          </div>
          <div class="oe-redline-item oe-redline-kept">
            <span class="oe-redline-num">6.</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-name">Trespass to Chattels</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-tag">Kept &rarr; Count 4</span>
          </div>
          <div class="oe-redline-item oe-redline-dropped">
            <span class="oe-redline-num">7.</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-name">Mass. G.L. Ch. 93A (Unfair Competition)</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-tag">Dropped</span>
          </div>
          <div class="oe-redline-item oe-redline-dropped">
            <span class="oe-redline-num">8.</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-name">Lanham Act</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-tag">Dropped</span>
          </div>
          <div class="oe-redline-item oe-redline-dropped">
            <span class="oe-redline-num">9.</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-name">Common Law Unfair Competition</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-tag">Dropped</span>
          </div>
          <div class="oe-redline-item oe-redline-dropped">
            <span class="oe-redline-num">10.</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-name">Defamation</span>
            <span class="oe-redline-tag">Dropped</span>
          </div>
          <div class="oe-redline-note">The defined term "OpenEvidence Trade Secrets" does not appear in the amended complaint. The phrase "trade secret information" remains once, in the factual allegations (para 78).</div>
        </div>
        <div class="oe-redline-footer">No filing explains why six causes of action were dropped.</div>
      </div>
      <p>Doximity had argued, in its September 15, 2025 memorandum supporting dismissal of the original complaint, that OpenEvidence's system prompt was not a protectable trade secret. The prompt consisted of "a brief set of instructions expressed in natural language, not proprietary code," Doximity wrote. The features OpenEvidence highlighted were "immediately observable to its users." Some companies publish their system prompts, the memo noted, citing Anthropic. NPI numbers, it pointed out, are publicly available through CMS.gov. And the complaint itself had conceded that "Defendants were not able to obtain the full system prompt." Doximity also alleged that OpenEvidence's own personnel had accessed competitor Pathway's platform using "placeholder or non-valid NPIs," naming four OpenEvidence employees. The memo characterized OpenEvidence's litigation strategy as serial, citing two parallel suits — one against Pathway in D. Mass. and one against Veracity-Health in the Northern District of California.</p>
      <p>Whether those arguments drove the amendment is unknown. In their January 9, 2026 reply brief, defendants offered their own characterization: "OpenEvidence — fully aware of the underlying facts from the outset — has abandoned any allegation that Doximity or Pathway stole its 'crown jewel' trade secrets by conversing with its chatbot." The word "abandoned" is defendants'. No court adopted it.</p>
      <p>Between the original complaint and the amendment, one other development: in August 2025, Doximity acquired Pathway Medical for $63 million. OpenEvidence voluntarily dismissed the separate Pathway suit without prejudice, consolidating those allegations into this case.</p>
      <p>The computer-fraud theory that would carry the case forward was not about what was typed into the chat box. It was about how the defendants allegedly got through the login page.</p>

    </section>

    <div class="phantom-wrap">
      <canvas class="openevidence-doximity-prompt-injection-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
    </div>
    <p class="canvas-note"><strong>The Credential</strong> — A bare particle absorbs a borrowed identity dot and crosses a dim threshold, which flares at contact; inside, its tendrils reach a glowing core and dissolve; the threshold flare grows brighter as the core dims, because the law reached the lie at the login, not the words in the prompt box.</p>
    <section class="section" id="the-ruling">
      <h2><span class="num">03 — The Ruling</span>The Lie at the Login</h2>
      <p>On January 22, 2026, Judge Stearns ruled on the motions to dismiss.</p>
      <p>The CFAA claim survived. The court's reasoning, verbatim from the electronic order (Dkt 86):</p>
      <blockquote class="pull-quote">"Defendants argue that dismissal of the CFAA claim is warranted because OpenEvidence necessarily gave them authorization to use the non-public portions of its system when it allowed their accounts to access these areas. The court is not convinced. The Amended Complaint alleges defendants used fraud to gain that access. At least one Circuit has found a defendant's access to non-public areas of a computer system 'without authorization' where the defendant provided false information to obtain that access. See United States v. Cuomo, 125 F.4th 354, 365 (2d Cir. 2025)."<cite>Judge Richard G. Stearns, electronic order, Jan. 22, 2026 (Dkt 86)</cite></blockquote>
      <p>A caveat: Cuomo is a Second Circuit decision. This case is in the District of Massachusetts, which sits in the First Circuit. Cuomo was persuasive authority, not binding precedent.</p>
      <p>Breach of contract survived. Access to the non-public tier required affirmatively clicking "I have read and agree to the Terms of Service," which the court called "a textbook contract of adhesion." Unjust enrichment survived as alternative pleading. Trespass to chattels was dismissed. The complaint, the court wrote, "fails to plausibly allege sufficient facts for the court to infer that OpenEvidence was somehow dispossessed of its system."</p>
      <p>This was a pleading-stage ruling — a motion to dismiss under Rule 12(b)(6). The court did not hold that the fraud theory was correct. It held the claim was plausibly alleged. The difference matters: at this stage, the court takes the plaintiff's allegations as true and asks only whether they state a claim. No evidence was weighed.</p>
      <p>The same day, a separate order (Dkt 87) addressed counterclaims. Doximity's Lanham Act counterclaim survived. Doximity alleged that OpenEvidence "created LinkedIn 'puppet accounts' to post negative stories about Doximity," publicly posted "misleading data" about Doximity's stock performance, and "misrepresented how many of its users are verified, whether the system has ever hallucinated, and whether the system scored 100% on a licensing exam." OpenEvidence disputed whether these statements were actionable, calling them "not deceptive in context," "mere puffery," or "true." The court found the factual disputes premature and let the claims proceed. Doximity's defamation and Chapter 93A counterclaims also survived. Pathway's Lanham Act and Chapter 93A counterclaims were dismissed for failure to identify specific false statements about Pathway.</p>
      <p>These too are adversarial allegations, not established facts.</p>
      <p>On the computer-fraud claim, what the ruling turned on was fraud at the point of access: the alleged use of real physicians' NPI credentials by people who are not physicians. It did not reach whether asking a chatbot to recite its system prompt is hacking or theft. That question was never decided.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="the-endgame">
      <h2><span class="num">04 — The Endgame</span>What the Slack Forgot</h2>
      <p>The court had stayed proceedings from June 30 through August 31, 2026, while the parties tried mediation. The stay ended without a settlement. On August 31, Judge Stearns set a new pretrial schedule: fact discovery due November 10, 2026.</p>
      <p>On September 17, 2026, the court ordered OpenEvidence to produce "responsive text messages and slack communications from any of the eight custodians identified by Doximity for the period of June 1, 2023 through February 6, 2026" within twenty-one days.</p>
      <p>Seven days later, OpenEvidence's lead counsel, Quinn Emanuel partner Stephen Broome, wrote a letter to the court. OpenEvidence, he stated, "will not comply with the Order in three respects." It would collect email from only three of eight custodians. It would limit the date range. It would not produce any text messages. The rationale: OpenEvidence feared that Doximity's counsel "may nevertheless communicate trade secrets from the documents to their clients." Broome acknowledged the court had already addressed this concern by allowing Attorneys' Eyes Only designations. OpenEvidence disagreed that this was sufficient.</p>
      <p>Then the second disclosure. "OpenEvidence's forensic vendor has determined that OpenEvidence's Slack data for June 1, 2023 through February 6, 2026 cannot be recovered."</p>
      <p>The letter closed with a statement unusual in federal litigation: "OpenEvidence further understands that, in taking this position, it will not be in compliance with the Order, and that consequences will follow."</p>
      <p>The next day, defendants filed a sanctions motion. "OpenEvidence has decided that a federal court's order is optional," began the memorandum from Skadden Arps. Defendants sought dismissal of OpenEvidence's claims, default judgment on their counterclaims, and attorneys' fees. The core allegation: OpenEvidence had demanded Slack preservation from every defendant — a December 19, 2024 letter from Goodwin Procter to Pathway covering "all instant messages, messages and channels utilized via Slack," threatening "case-termination sanctions" for failure to comply; and a June 3, 2025 letter from Quinn Emanuel to Doximity covering "emails, Slack channels, text messages" — but "did not preserve its own."</p>
      <p>What happened to the Slack data became the central dispute of the case's final month. The two sides told different stories.</p>
      <p>Defendants' version, filed October 5 in their accounting motion (Dkt 140): OpenEvidence's workspace settings deleted public-channel messages after 75 days and private-channel and direct messages after 30 days. "Those settings were not Slack's default, which on paid plans is indefinite retention. Someone at OpenEvidence, at some point replaced Slack's default with 30- and 75-day deletion." A second loss: "custodians' work messages on an unidentified messaging application were not retained."</p>
      <p>OpenEvidence's version, filed October 14 in its opposition (Dkt 158): The workspace used Slack's Enterprise Select plan. The retention settings "were in place before any dispute with Pathway or Doximity arose," according to CEO Daniel Nadler's declaration. All 671 channels used workspace-default retention. No past exports existed. Slack's legal-hold feature was not used. A forensic consultant confirmed the data was unrecoverable. OpenEvidence issued a written litigation hold "on or around June 9, 2025." The Nadler declaration, OpenEvidence argued, "answers every question Defendants claim remains unanswered."</p>
      <p>The court never ruled on whether the deletion was culpable. No spoliation finding was entered.</p>
      <p>On October 5, Judge Stearns denied the sanctions motion as moot: "OpenEvidence has indicated its intent to comply with the court's Order, see Dkt # 136, superseding the representation upon which defendants' motion is based."</p>
      <div class="oe-docket-wrap">
        <div class="oe-docket-header">The Endgame: September 17 to October 16, 2026</div>
